#{[17592186550749 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -4 & -2 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550753 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550756 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550764 :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186550765 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550773 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.345 \\\\ 0.655\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550774 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550776 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550784 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 19 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 14 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ -9 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ -11 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550785 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550786 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550791 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}(-3)^{2} \\\\ (-1)^{2} \\\\ {1}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2} \\\\ {5}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550795 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.12 \\\\ 0.52 \\\\ 0.36\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550802 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550804 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -25 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 35\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550805 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550821 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550823 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550825 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550831 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550844 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550849 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550856 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550863 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550871 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550877 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550878 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 12\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550880 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550888 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 0 & {0}^{2} \\\\ 19 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 14 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ -9 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ -11 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550890 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.433 \\\\ 0.567\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550892 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.24 \\\\ 0.32 \\\\ 0.44\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550894 :answer/value "\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 9 & -1\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186550897 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550899 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550909 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550915 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550927 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550928 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550931 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550934 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.28 \\\\ 0.38 \\\\ 0.34\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550938 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -4 & 2 & 5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550940 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550950 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550952 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550957 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550962 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550963 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550979 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550980 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550983 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550987 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550996 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186550998 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551000 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0.289 \\\\ 0.711\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551019 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 & -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551028 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551029 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551036 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551037 :answer/value "$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $P=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186551051 :answer/value "-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}"] [17592186551057 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186551239 :question/problem "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}113 \\\\ 414\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [17592186551239 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}113 \\\\ 414\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."] [17592186551242 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{3}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={(|3-1{|}^{3}+|2-0{|}^{3})}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={16}^{1/3} \\\\  & ≈2.520.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551243 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each decade: \n\n- $12\\%$ of people who live in the cities relocate to the countryside. This implies that $88\\%$ of them remain living in the cities.\n\n- $28\\%$ of people who live in the countryside relocate to the cities. This implies that $72\\%$ of them remain living in the countryside.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.88 & 0.28 \\\\ 0.12 & 0.72\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551244 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186551244 :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186551247 :question/problem "Calculate the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*"] [17592186551247 :question/worked-solution "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{14}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -13 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -13 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -13 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -13 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6(35-(-9)) \\\\  & =-264.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551259 :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551259 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551261 :question/worked-solution "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of flour is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}\\begin{bmatrix}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0.42 \\\\ 0.58\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $42\\%$ of customers will use flour $A.$ Computing $42\\%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.42=4\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,200$ customers will use flour $A$ in one month."] [17592186551262 :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,X=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551262 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $T$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $U$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $X$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $U$ and $X$ are elementary matrices."] [17592186551266 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551266 :question/worked-solution "We write down $L$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[L\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551268 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186551268 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-1){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-3){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -2 & -6\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -2 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551271 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186551271 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{33}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551272 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{4}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={‖\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={(|4-5{|}^{4}+|3-3{|}^{4}+|1-4{|}^{4})}^{1/4} \\\\  & ={82}^{1/4} \\\\  & ≈3.009.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551278 :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551278 :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551279 :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551282 :question/problem "$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{41}$ equal to zero.*"] [17592186551282 :question/worked-solution "Let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{41}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix} &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186551286 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle N=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551286 :question/worked-solution "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12} & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:=12{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551290 :question/problem "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}0.45 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix},\\,W=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551290 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}0.1 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\begin{aligned}0.1+0.9=1 \\\\ 0.7+0.3=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.45 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.65\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$  *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ only.\""] [17592186551291 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*"] [17592186551291 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -30\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=-3-6x.$"] [17592186551292 :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2} & {8}^{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\beta =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551294 :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,N=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551294 :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $N$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is not an elementary matrix."] [17592186551295 :question/problem "There are three supermarket chains in a particular city: chain $A,$ chain $B,$ and chain $C.$ A study has shown that, each month\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $A,$ $5\\%$ change to chain $B$ and $15\\%$ change to chain $C,$\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $B,$ $25\\%$ change to chain $A$ and $25\\%$ change to chain $C,$\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $C,$ $15\\%$ change to chain $A$ and $35\\%$ change to chain $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & a & 0.15 \\\\ b & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & c\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186551295 :question/worked-solution "We are given the following situation for each month:\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $A,$ $5\\%$ change to chain $B$ and $15\\%$ change to chain $C.$ This implies that $80\\%$ of them continue to prefer chain $A.$\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $B,$ $25\\%$ change to chain $A$ and $25\\%$ change to chain $C.$ This implies that $50\\%$ of them continue to prefer chain $B.$\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $C,$ $15\\%$ change to chain $A$ and $35\\%$ change to chain $B.$ This implies that $50\\%$ of them continue to prefer chain $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.8 & 0.25 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.05 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & 0.5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.05+0.5=0.8.\n$$"] [17592186551296 :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551296 :question/worked-solution "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186551298 :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$"]}
